lorentz-transformation-06653544.conceptual_definition:
A contravariant four-vector, $A^\mu$, is formally defined as any set of four quantities that, under a change of inertial reference frame, transform according to the rule $A'^\mu = \Lambda^\mu_{\;
u} A^
u$. Here, $A^
u$ are the components in the original frame $S$, $A'^\mu$ are the components in the new frame $S'$, and $\Lambda^\mu_{\;
u}$ is the Lorentz transformation matrix. This transformation law ensures that physical laws written in terms of four-vectors maintain their form in all inertial frames.

